WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:09.210
So earlier when we were talking about the parts of the mainframe, we looked at where the CPU and the processors are, right?

2
00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:15.910
And that's called the CPC or central processor complex. We also saw where all the IO adapters are.

3
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.410
Did you wonder at all where all the hard drives are?

4
00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:27.110
In a mainframe environment, disk storage is handled by something called direct access storage devices, commonly called DASDI.

5
00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:34.110
These are big, almost mainframe sized units, stuff full of hard drives, both mechanical and solid state.

6
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:44.110
They can provide the disk storage for mainframe. They can actually provide disk storage for many mainframes, and many DASDI can be connected to a single mainframe.

7
00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.110
So you can see there's lots of ways to connect these things.

8
00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:56.110
In order to make these connections to devices like DASDI and others, we need to define the logical paths through the physical connections, so it all works.

9
00:00:56.110 --> 00:01:08.110
Things are better when they work, right? The iOS CDS, which stands for the input-output configuration dataset, represents the connection between the resources and the systems that use them.

10
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:14.110
Resources are things like channels, icons, switches, control units, and other devices.

11
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:22.110
Basically, anything outside of the mainframe that we want to connect to, and the stuff inside the mainframe that we're going to use to connect to it.

12
00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:30.110
So when you define an IO configuration, you provide the physical as well as the logical information about those resources.

13
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:35.110
In the mainframe, there's an input-output subsystem, or IO subsystem.

14
00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:42.110
Now this controls the channel operations. That requires the information about the hardware IO configuration.

15
00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:49.110
To define this, we use a program called the input-output configuration program, or IOCP.

16
00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:57.110
The IOCDS is built through a series of statements. You build it brick by brick, and once you build it, the IOCDS gets stored on the SEs.

17
00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:03.110
Those are those computers within the computer, and then those are picked up when the system gets powered on.

18
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:10.110
To use IOCP to build an IOCDS, you need to know about four things, and we're going to find them here.

19
00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:16.110
First, you need to know what the L-Pars or partitions are that you want to work with. That's easy enough.

20
00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:21.110
We've been working with those for a while now. Then you need to know about the channels.

21
00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:26.110
Channels are the links between the L-Pars and the physical ports on the IO cards.

22
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:30.110
So we've got channel paths. Each one has an ID.

23
00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:34.110
C-H-P-I-D, pronounced as Chippet.

24
00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:39.110
We're defining a logical connection on there with the Chippet, and the channel path

25
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:44.110
exits the mainframe on the physical ID, or a P-Chid.

26
00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:49.110
Control units are attached to channel paths, and they're what actually drives the device we're connecting to.

27
00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:55.110
Having a dedicated control unit that is really good at handling devices like disk or tape,

28
00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:00.110
means the mainframe operating system doesn't have to worry about all those deep down details.

29
00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:02.110
That's a good thing.

30
00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.110
And finally, there's the input-output device.

31
00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:09.110
The thing we're actually trying to talk to, which the controlling unit is tasked with controlling.

32
00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.110
If you know those four things, you're ready to start building an I-O-C-D-S.

33
00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.110
So let's take a look at those statements.

34
00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:18.110
There's five I-O-C-P statements.

35
00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:23.110
ID, resource, chippet, control unit, and I-O device.

36
00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:28.110
ID is optional, but it helps in organizing so you can find things easier later on.

37
00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.110
You can also set the name of the local systems, the machine limits,

38
00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.110
and the rules to be enforced by the I-O-C-P.

39
00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.110
It comes first and can only be defined once.

40
00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:41.110
Up next is resource, and this one is required.

41
00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:45.110
It defines the L-par names, or if you're sharing channels between L-pars,

42
00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:51.110
you can also define what's called a multiple image facility image ID, or myth ID.

43
00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:56.110
So let me talk a little bit about the logical channel subsystems, or L-C-S-S.

44
00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:01.110
The channel subsystem is the world that all of the channels live in, right?

45
00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:06.110
The logical channel subsystem is a way of getting even more capacity for those chippets,

46
00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:10.110
because now we have four logical worlds for them to live in.

47
00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:13.110
Because we can create up to four of those logical channel subsystems,

48
00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:16.110
we get four times the amount of chippets.

49
00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.110
This just gives us a whole lot more options for how we can partition our resources.

50
00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:24.110
You can also use this area to define a few dynamic options,

51
00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.110
which we won't get into here.

52
00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:30.110
Now, you get one resource statement, and it needs to come right after the ID statement,

53
00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.110
if you have one.

54
00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.110
It includes every logical partition in the configuration.

55
00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:38.110
Up next is Chippet.

56
00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.110
The Chippet statement defines all the channels on the system.

57
00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:46.110
These are the logical connections between the L-par and the physical P-shit.

58
00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:51.110
It defines whether that channel is dedicated, shared, reconfigurable, or spanned.

59
00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.110
And if you're connected to Fycon switch, it also handles the assignments as well.

60
00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:59.110
It also determines the availability of chippets to L-pars.

61
00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:04.110
These go in after the resource and before the next two types of statements.

62
00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.110
Number four is the control unit.

63
00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:12.110
Now, this is another optional statement, but you need it if you need to connect to a device through a control unit.

64
00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.110
And it describes three things.

65
00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.110
First, the characteristics of the control unit.

66
00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:25.110
It also defines the channel paths that the control unit is attached to, and the unit addresses that the control unit recognizes.

67
00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:32.110
So basically, how I'm going to connect to the control unit, what it is I'm trying to connect to, that channel path I'm using to connect to it,

68
00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:35.110
and how many unit addresses we're identifying.

69
00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.110
Lastly, there's the I-O device.

70
00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:42.110
Now, this is another optional statement, but you need it if you actually want to talk to something.

71
00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.110
You need to have, first, the I-O device number.

72
00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:51.110
You also need the device characteristics and the control units to which the device is assigned.

73
00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:55.110
Now, this is all fairly abstract at this point, and it's one thing to talk about it.

74
00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:59.110
Let's see what it actually happens once we start defining things with these statements.

75
00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:06.110
What we're going to be doing with the following five statements is building a connection between an L-par through a chippet,

76
00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:12.110
out a p-tude through a phi-con unit, and out to a 3390-DASD unit.

77
00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.110
We'll start with the ID statement.

78
00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:18.110
The symbol and everything else in the brackets is optional.

79
00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.110
The ID here says this is an ID statement.

80
00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:25.110
Message one here has the identifier of the I-O-C-D-S.

81
00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:31.110
This also gets printed out on the first line of the heading of the I-O-C-P configuration reports.

82
00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:34.110
It makes it easier to identify it.

83
00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:41.110
Message two here has information on when it was updated, which is a pretty good idea to have that kind of stuff in there.

84
00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:44.110
And you see that X all the way on the right side there?

85
00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:48.110
That means that this line is continued on the line below it.

86
00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:54.110
System specifies the machine limits and rules that I-O-C-P enforces for verification,

87
00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:58.110
and there's also this L system and T-O-K.

88
00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:02.110
These are enclosed in braces, which means that they represent alternative items.

89
00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:04.110
So, they're optional.

90
00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:09.110
And because we already defined system, we don't have to also define these other two.

91
00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:14.110
Here's an example of a resource statement, which again identifies resources.

92
00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:18.110
So, first we've got the partition, or part as it's labeled in here,

93
00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:22.110
which defines the channel's subsystem ID or CSS.

94
00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:26.110
Then we've got the MIF ID, which is used for sharing chippets between L-Pars.

95
00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:31.110
Now, we're not doing that here, so it's not in our example, but that's where you would put it.

96
00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.110
And then we have three L-Pars defined here.

97
00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:38.110
L-P-1, L-P-2, and L-P-3.

98
00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:42.110
You'll see that L-P-1 was assigned an image ID of two.

99
00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:45.110
L-P-3 has an image ID of five.

100
00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:50.110
And because we didn't specify one for L-P-2, it will be assigned one arbitrarily.

101
00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.110
Here's the chippet statement.

102
00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:58.110
We start out by defining channel path ID-35 in channel subsystem-1.

103
00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:04.110
For the card port associated with physical channel, that's P-Chid ID-100.

104
00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:08.110
We can tell it's a ficon card by the type, Equaling FC.

105
00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:12.110
And it's shared between logical partitions L-P-1 and L-P-2.

106
00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:14.110
Remember those from earlier?

107
00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:16.110
It's all starting to come together, right?

108
00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:20.110
And we can also see that this is associated with switch three E-Z.

109
00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:22.110
Up next is Control Unit.

110
00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:26.110
Now, what you're looking at here is the assignment of Control Unit,

111
00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:34.110
number 26 Baker-0 to an IBM storage controller type 3990.

112
00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:39.110
And the link address of E-Z-0 coming out of the ficon switch.

113
00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:43.110
And on the main frame side, that's connected to channel path 35.

114
00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:46.110
It also defines a logical address of one.

115
00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:51.110
And this gives us the unit addresses of zero zero through zero fox.

116
00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:55.110
Translate zero fox from hex to decimal, that's 15.

117
00:08:55.110 --> 00:09:00.110
So we get 16 addresses from this statement because we start counting from zero.

118
00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:04.110
So now we've defined all we need to know about the Control Unit.

119
00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.110
One more.

120
00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:07.110
Let's take a look at the I-O device statement.

121
00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.110
So here it is, just so you know what it is.

122
00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:15.110
This defines 16 device numbers, starting with address 8100.

123
00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:21.110
This part right here assigns devices to Control Unit, number 26 Baker-0,

124
00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.110
on an IBM 3990.

125
00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:29.110
That 3990 Control Unit manages the device, which is a 3390.

126
00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:32.110
So two very similar numbers.

127
00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:36.110
And you would absolutely not be the first person in the world to mix them up.

128
00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:38.110
In fact, you'd be in pretty good company.

129
00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:40.110
Hi, I'm Jeff Bisty.

130
00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.110
I believe we've met.

131
00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:47.110
Lastly, it assigns a channel subsystem to L-Pars 1, 2, and 3.

132
00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:51.110
This is what the L-Pars are actually going to use to connect to the channels.

133
00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:54.110
So what do we actually just do?

134
00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:59.110
We've got chip at 35, which is associated with P-CID 100.

135
00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:02.110
And we know it's a 5-con channel, right?

136
00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:06.110
We've also defined this 5-con switch with an address of 3EZ.

137
00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:11.110
We've also defined the switch is connected to chip at 35 on the main frame side,

138
00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:15.110
and it exits on link EZ-0.

139
00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:20.110
And then it connects to the DS8886 DASDY.

140
00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:22.110
Whole lot disks in there.

141
00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.110
It's got to control unit inside of it.

142
00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:28.110
That's the 3990 we defined in the last two statements.

143
00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:33.110
And we're connecting to Control Unit number 26 Baker-0,

144
00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:39.110
and it's giving us addresses 8100 through 8100 Fox.

145
00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:41.110
That was exhausting.

146
00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:42.110
I know.

147
00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:44.110
It wasn't exactly plug and play.

148
00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:47.110
But the reason we basically define every little thing

149
00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:51.110
and how every little thing connects to every other little thing

150
00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:54.110
is because there needs to be absolutely no two ways

151
00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:57.110
of interpreting these definitions.

152
00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:59.110
It's also actually kind of nice,

153
00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:02.110
because in this document, in this series of statements,

154
00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:05.110
the entire IO ecosystem is defined.

155
00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:09.110
So you can read through one of these and get an extremely clear picture

156
00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.110
of how your addresses are defined,

157
00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:14.110
what they're connected to, which L parts get access to them,

